/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Sequelize is one of the oldest Node.js ORMs. It uses a class-based approach where you define models in JavaScript. It lacks built-in TypeScript support and auto-complete. Prisma uses a dedicated schema file and generates a fully typed client automatically.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Prisma vs TypeORM)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (TypeORM uses decorators and classes \(like Java-style ORM\). It has TypeScript support but requires lots of boilerplate code. Prisma is simpler: you write a schema, and everything else is generated. Less code, fewer bugs.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Prisma vs Drizzle)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Drizzle is a newer, lightweight ORM that is closer to raw SQL. It gives more control but requires more SQL knowledge. Prisma abstracts SQL away completely, making it better for beginners who do not know SQL yet.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Prisma vs Raw SQL)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Writing raw SQL gives maximum control but zero safety nets. No auto-complete, no type checking, easy to make typos. Prisma gives you 90% of SQL's power with 10% of the effort and full safety guarantees.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
